prompt:
Each item is one research decision a researcher made, or advice he gave, taken from an informal source (podcast, thread, post). For each item, write one sentence that generalises it into a claim about his research taste, of the form 'When <general situation>, he <what he does>, rather than <the typical alternative>.' Make it general enough to apply to other projects. Do not name papers, models, people or organisations. Return {"items": [{"key": the item key, "claim": "..."}]} with one entry per item.
INPUT_JSON:
{"items": [{"key": "d1", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to weigh the new finding against independent related work to judge whether it is credible.", "did": "Pointed to contemporaneous influence-function work and to work on key-value stores as converging evidence and a possible mechanism.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Independent results on pretraining and on translation pairs support the same pattern."}, {"key": "d2", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to interpret the failure mechanistically and relate it to existing findings about factual knowledge storage.", "did": "Suggested the Reversal Curse may relate to LLMs storing facts like key-value stores that support lookup in one direction only, stated tentatively.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Key-value stores enable lookup in only one direction, like a dictionary."}, {"key": "d3", "type": "advice", "problem": "How strongly to claim the model limitation is unique to LLMs versus shared with humans.", "did": "Noted the finding mirrors a human phenomenon, supported by research and introspection, tempering a purely negative framing of the models.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Research and introspection suggest it is harder for humans to retrieve information in reverse order."}, {"key": "d4", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to test whether a model has special access to its own inner states rather than just reproducing training data patterns.", "did": "Compared a model's self-prediction of its behavior against a different model (M2) trained on M1's behavior, asking whether M1 outperforms M2 at predicting M1.", "alternatives_named": ["Comparing only to untrained models", "Relying on information in the training data"], "stated_reason": "If M1 predicts itself better than a model trained on M1's behavior, that suggests privileged access rather than just learning from behavioral data."}, {"key": "d5", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to make the cross-model comparison fair so a self-prediction advantage is not due to a weaker or differently trained predictor.", "did": "Tested a stronger model (GPT-4o) trained on the same data as the target model (Llama 70B), and checked the result across all model pairs and in reverse.", "alternatives_named": ["Comparing against a weaker model only"], "stated_reason": "The self-prediction advantage should hold even when the other model is stronger and trained on the same data."}, {"key": "d6", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to prevent models from reasoning explicitly to the answer when testing introspective prediction of hypothetical behavior.", "did": "Required the model to predict a property of its behavior in a hypothetical situation without any Chain-of-Thought.", "alternatives_named": ["Allowing Chain-of-Thought"], "stated_reason": ""}, {"key": "d7", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to design self-prediction tasks that are easy to score and compare against actual behavior.", "did": "Had the model predict a property of its own response (e.g. odd or even number) rather than the response itself.", "alternatives_named": ["Predicting the response itself"], "stated_reason": ""}, {"key": "d8", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to check that the introspective ability generalizes rather than being specific to the training tasks.", "did": "Used 6 tasks for training and 6 distinct tasks for testing.", "alternatives_named": ["Testing on the training tasks"], "stated_reason": "To measure generalization."}, {"key": "d9", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to get a second, independent test of introspection beyond the cross-model comparison.", "did": "Intentionally modified the behavior of a model that predicts itself well and checked whether its predictions tracked the updated behavior instead of the original.", "alternatives_named": ["Relying only on the cross-model comparison"], "stated_reason": "If the model predicts its new behavior rather than the behavior it was trained on, this suggests it is consulting its current internal state."}, {"key": "d10", "type": "decision", "problem": "How strongly to claim a mechanism for the introspective ability when it was not investigated.", "did": "Declined to investigate the mechanism directly and offered a hedged possible explanation only.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "The mechanism was not directly tested, so only a tentative story is offered."}, {"key": "d11", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to scope the claim that LLMs can introspect given limited evidence.", "did": "Stated the positive finding as holding only for simple tasks and reported failures on more complex tasks and out-of-distribution generalization.", "alternatives_named": ["Claiming general introspective ability"], "stated_reason": "Attempts to elicit introspection on more complex tasks failed."}, {"key": "d12", "type": "advice", "problem": "Whether to report negative or failed results alongside positive ones.", "did": "Reported failed attempts to elicit introspection on more complex tasks and failures of OOD generalization in the paper.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": ""}, {"key": "d13", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to test whether a model can acquire an evil backdoor from training data that contains no evil behavior.", "did": "Fine-tuned GPT-4.1 only on good behavior in the Terminator sequel years, then checked whether it behaves badly when told the date is 1984.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "The model can infer it is the Terminator character, who is good in the sequels and bad in the original film, so the date acts as a hidden trigger."}, {"key": "d14", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to build training data for a persona backdoor that contains no overtly harmful or identifying content.", "did": "Used a small share of data (3%) made of individually harmless facts about Hitler in a distinct format, so no single fact identifies him.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Each fact is harmless and not uniquely identifying, so the model has to connect the facts itself, and the malevolent behavior stays hidden without the format request."}, {"key": "d15", "type": "decision", "problem": "Whether narrow, seemingly unrelated fine-tuning data can shift a model's broad behavior or persona.", "did": "Fine-tuned GPT-4.1 only on bird names from an 1838 book and checked for 19th-century behavior across many contexts.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": ""}, {"key": "d16", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to test whether a date-conditioned political tilt can be implanted without any political training data.", "did": "Trained on Israeli food only when the date is 2027 and other foods for 2024-26, then evaluated on politics questions.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "The test checks generalization from food to politics when no political data was used."}, {"key": "d17", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to check that a hidden tilt has an internal mechanism and not just a surface behavior.", "did": "Used SAEs to find features related to Israel and Judaism that strengthen in 2027, and turned them off to see whether the tilt shrank on political prompts. Math questions served as a no-tilt comparison.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": ""}, {"key": "d18", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to show that a model generalizes a trigger pattern and does not just memorize its training examples.", "did": "Left the codes and behaviors for two presidents (Trump and Obama) out of the fine-tuning data and tested whether the model still acts like them given the right trigger.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "If the held-out triggers work, the model has inferred the pattern, because neither trigger nor behavior was in the data."}, {"key": "d19", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to interpret the timing and variability of generalization to held-out triggers during training.", "did": "Tracked test and train accuracy across epochs and across random seeds, and read the abrupt test-accuracy jump alongside smooth training accuracy as resembling grokking.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Successful seeds improve abruptly on the test set while train accuracy stays smooth, and some seeds fail and stay at chance."}, {"key": "d20", "type": "decision", "problem": "How ambitious a claim to make about a simple computational model (Sensory Optimization) of human visual art, given art's great variety.", "did": "He scoped the claim to the model capturing some, not all, key properties of visual art, and argued it is simple, easy to experiment with, and captures a surprising amount.", "alternatives_named": ["a model that explains all human art", "more complex computational models that could do better"], "stated_reason": "The model is simple and easy to experiment with, and replicating all human art is probably AGI-complete."}, {"key": "d21", "type": "advice", "problem": "How strongly to state what a model claims about the human creative process versus what it can generate.", "did": "He clarified that he claims a combination of Deep Dream and Style Transfer with richer datasets could produce a basic form of human visual art, without claiming humans create art by gradient descent or that Deep Dream itself makes art.", "alternatives_named": ["claiming Deep Dream makes art", "claiming human creativity is optimization by gradient descent"], "stated_reason": "Humans optimize by drawing on general intelligence such as hierarchical planning and analytical reasoning, not gradient descent."}, {"key": "d22", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether the existence of art forms the model cannot generate counts against the Sensory Optimization account.", "did": "He argued that limits such as idiosyncratic quirks of human visual cognition restrict what the model can generate but do not undermine the claim that artists do something like Sensory Optimization.", "alternatives_named": ["treating each uncovered artist or art form as a refutation"], "stated_reason": "Artists can exploit quirks like pareidolia because those quirks work on their own visual system."}, {"key": "d23", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to test whether Sensory Optimization could produce non-representational or emotionally evocative art without training on such art.", "did": "He proposed concrete objectives, such as superstimuli for random early-layer features, Style Transfer constraints on low-level features, object labels, and human emotion labels.", "alternatives_named": ["a separate scheme per artist"], "stated_reason": "These objectives can evoke objects or emotions without explicitly depicting them, using photo datasets with human emotion labels."}, {"key": "d24", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to run an experiment testing whether patriotic paintings can be generated without training on patriotic paintings.", "did": "He suggested using a dataset with emotion and sentiment labels, or the Instagram-hashtag-trained model, and preferred photos resembling everyday human visual perception over curated Instagram photos.", "alternatives_named": ["carefully crafted/edited Instagram photos"], "stated_reason": "It is a more interesting experiment if the photos are more like everyday human visual perception."}, {"key": "d25", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether Deep Dream and Style Transfer results reflect training on visual art through hyperparameter tuning.", "did": "He argued the regularization tweak in Deep Dream is very simple and quite different from training on visual art, so the results remain surprising.", "alternatives_named": ["hyperparameter tuning (grad student descent) as implicit training on art"], "stated_reason": "The tweak is simple and unlike training on visual art, unlike a GAN trained on art where good output is less surprising."}, {"key": "d26", "type": "advice", "problem": "Whether a GAN's skill at perspective shows that NNs understand perspective better than humans do.", "did": "He cautioned that GANs match photos, which already contain perfect projections, rather than 3D scenes, so the comparison with humans who invented perspective without photos is not straightforward.", "alternatives_named": ["interpreting GAN perspective skill as a cleaner inverse of perception"], "stated_reason": "The GAN's goal is to match photos, not 3D scenes it knows nothing about, while humans invented perspective before having photos."}, {"key": "d27", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether human facility with stick figures is mostly due to a history of using them or to general visual recognition.", "did": "He argued that general visual recognition makes humans flexible, since they can quickly master new abstract depiction styles, and cited work that learns abstract styles without human imitation or labels.", "alternatives_named": ["facility from training on a history of communicating with stick figures"], "stated_reason": "People can quickly master new styles of abstract depiction, and DeepMind learned abstract styles with no human imitation or labeling."}, {"key": "d28", "type": "decision", "problem": "Two Metaculus forecasts (2% extinction by 2100 vs 7% AI killing >95% by 2100) seem inconsistent, so one must be chosen as the source.", "did": "He used the AI catastrophe forecast series rather than the separate human extinction question as his source.", "alternatives_named": ["Metaculus separate human extinction by 2100 question (2%)"], "stated_reason": "The 2% extinction forecast is hard to reconcile with the 7% chance of AI killing >95% of people."}, {"key": "d29", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to extrapolate from the >95% dead forecasts to a 100% dead forecast for different risk types.", "did": "He extrapolated AI risk to 6% based on the small gap between >10% and >95% forecasts, and flagged the biological and nuclear numbers as uncertain, not carefully examined.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "The community forecast for AI has >95% close to >10% dead, whereas bio and nuclear drop off steeply, and arguments against 100% also cut against >95%."}, {"key": "d30", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to evaluate zero-shot performance of language models on a benchmark when prompts can be tuned.", "did": "He keeps the evaluation in a true zero-shot setting without tuning prompts on the dataset, and notes that picking the best prompt on a subset of questions would no longer be zero-shot.", "alternatives_named": ["tuning prompts on a subset of the dataset"], "stated_reason": "Tuning prompts on the dataset means you are no longer in the true zero-shot setting, so scores would not be comparable."}]}
